1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,720
Virtual threads closed a long concurrency
arc. A quieter question has been waiting
since the beginning: when you run a
program, how do classes actually appear
inside the JVM?

2
00:00:10,900 --> 00:00:24,019
You write source, compile to bytecode, and
somehow main runs. Between "bytecode on
disk" and "code the CPU can execute" sits
class loading — lazy, layered, and
responsible for a family of mysterious
errors.

3
00:00:24,140 --> 00:00:33,579
Classes appear lazily. Understanding
loaders explains ClassNotFoundException
and NoClassDefFoundError mysteries that
look identical until you know what failed
when.

4
00:00:33,700 --> 00:00:44,940
Start from a deliberate load: Look at this
java for a moment. Class.forName asks the
JVM to load, link, and initialize the
named class if needed, then returns a
Class object.

5
00:00:45,060 --> 00:00:54,460
Printing the class loader shows which
loader defined it. Classes are not all
dumped into one flat bag at startup. They
arrive through loaders with parents and
responsibilities.

6
00:00:54,579 --> 00:01:06,420
The usual hierarchy is bootstrap,
platform, and application loaders. The
bootstrap loader knows the most
fundamental JDK classes. The platform
loader covers other modular JDK content.

7
00:01:06,539 --> 00:01:15,619
The application loader finds your
classpath or module path types. Parent
delegation is the rule of thumb: ask the
parent loader first before loading a class
yourself.

8
00:01:15,739 --> 00:01:21,259
That keeps core types unique and prevents
user code from casually replacing String
with a rogue version.

9
00:01:21,379 --> 00:01:31,099
Custom loaders — plugin systems,
containers — complicate it, but the
delegation instinct remains: prefer
parents for shared types so identity stays
coherent.

10
00:01:31,219 --> 00:01:45,019
Two classes with the same binary name
loaded by different loaders are different
runtime types. That fact alone explains
entire categories of "but they look the
same!" cast failures in plugin
architectures.

11
00:01:45,140 --> 00:01:58,700
Loading is only one phase. Linking
verifies bytecode and prepares memory
structures. Verification checks that the
bytecode obeys JVM safety rules — stack
discipline, type constraints — so the
runtime can trust it.

12
00:01:58,819 --> 00:02:07,939
Initialization runs static initializers.
Forcing eager init everywhere can slow
startup; lazy init can defer surprises
until first use.

13
00:02:08,060 --> 00:02:17,300
Know that Class.forName and first active
use are moments when initialization can
run. Metaspace holds class metadata — not
your ordinary object heap.

14
00:02:17,420 --> 00:02:30,620
When you generate or load huge numbers of
classes, metaspace pressure can become its
own incident story. Dynamic loading has
costs: more classes, more metadata, more
chances for classpath order surprises.

15
00:02:30,740 --> 00:02:43,860
"Memory" is not only -Xmx. Classpath order
surprises are legendary. Two JARs contain
different versions of a type; the first
one wins. The code compiles against one
version and runs against another.

16
00:02:43,980 --> 00:02:54,860
ClassNotFoundException often means a
loader could not find the bytes.
NoClassDefFoundError often means the JVM
previously failed to initialize or link a
class that the compiler thought existed.

17
00:02:54,980 --> 00:03:05,580
The names are not interchangeable. A
container incident makes the loader story
concrete. The app JAR expects a library
class. The image's classpath omits that
JAR version.

18
00:03:05,700 --> 00:03:18,620
At compile time on a developer machine
everything resolved. At runtime first use
fails. Printing loaders and examining the
layered classpath turns "works on my
machine" into a diff you can fix.

19
00:03:18,740 --> 00:03:31,420
What if two plugins each ship a different
version of the same library and each uses
its own loader? Same names, different
types, confusing ClassCastException.
Identity is loader plus name, not name
alone.

20
00:03:31,540 --> 00:03:42,740
Dynamic proxies and generated subclasses
multiply classes at runtime. Frameworks do
this constantly. Each generated class
consumes metaspace and must be loadable
under the right loader.

21
00:03:42,860 --> 00:03:49,020
When a framework says "add this agent" or
"open this module," it is often speaking
loader and linkage language.

22
00:03:49,140 --> 00:03:58,860
Initialization order can also bite: static
fields that call code which loads more
classes which initialize more statics.
Keep static initializers boring.

23
00:03:58,980 --> 00:04:12,660
Picture an application that fails on first
request with NoClassDefFoundError for a
class that "is clearly in the JAR." Often
static initialization threw earlier, the
JVM marked the class failed,

24
00:04:12,780 --> 00:04:15,820
and later use reports
NoClassDefFoundError.

25
00:04:15,940 --> 00:04:19,860
Reading the first failure in the log
matters more than staring at the second.

26
00:04:19,980 --> 00:04:31,420
Hold a practical checklist: know which
loader owns your application types; treat
linkage errors as phase failures, not
random noise; watch metaspace when
generating classes;

27
00:04:31,540 --> 00:04:34,900
make classpath contents an explicit
deployment concern.

28
00:04:35,020 --> 00:04:40,420
The next natural hunger is what those
bytes actually contain — the JVM's
instruction language itself.
